# Line endings.
#
# Text files are normalised to LF in the repository and checked out with whatever the platform
# wants, which is the default and is fine for source.
*               text=auto

# Except for the files that are read by something other than a text editor.
#
# A shell script with CRLF line endings fails on Linux with "bad interpreter: No such file or
# directory" -- the carriage return becomes part of the interpreter's path. The error names the
# shell rather than the line endings, which makes it a slow thing to work out, and it only appears
# on a machine that cloned the repository with core.autocrlf on. That is every Windows clone, so
# the packaging script and the test lab's entrypoint would break for exactly the people who did not
# write them.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf

# systemd units and Debian control files are parsed by tools that are equally unimpressed by a
# stray carriage return.
*.service       text eol=lf
*.socket        text eol=lf

# Binary, so git neither converts them nor tries to diff them as text.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pfx           binary
*.snk           binary
